# REST API Conventions

Follow NestJS conventions and standard REST practices.

## HTTP Methods

- GET → retrieve (no side effects)
- POST → create
- PATCH → partial update
- PUT → full update
- DELETE → remove

Never use POST for everything. Refactor misuse to the correct method.

## URL Design

- Resources are **nouns**, plural: `/users`, `/users/:id`
- Never action-based routes: `/getUsers`, `/deleteUser`, `/updateUser`

## Parameters

| Location | Use for |
|----------|---------|
| Path (`:id`) | Identifying one resource |
| Query (`?`) | Filtering, search, pagination |
| Body | POST creation data, PATCH/PUT update data only |

Forbidden:
- DELETE or GET with a body
- IDs in body when they belong in the URL (`DELETE /users/1`, not `DELETE /users { id: 1 }`)

Decorators: `@Param()` for path, `@Query()` for query, `@Body()` for payload.

## NestJS Structure

- One controller per resource; keep controllers thin
- Business logic lives in services
- DTOs + `class-validator` for every POST / PATCH / PUT

## Status Codes

- 200 success · 201 created · 204 delete (no content) · 400 bad request · 404 not found

## Refactoring Checklist

When touching endpoints: fix POST misuse, move IDs body → URL, replace action routes with resources.

**Golden rule:** endpoint describes an action → wrong; describes a resource → correct.
